 ## Overview
-The Volume Partitioning Pipeline is a data preprocessing method that splits a 3D volume into disjoint foreground regions, optionally partitions them spatially or by intensity, crops them to bounding boxes, downsamples, and exports them as raw files with metadata [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of managing large 3D datasets by reducing memory footprint and enabling efficient out-of-core or chunked processing. It differs from its sibling [VolumeSegmenter Pipeline](11584-volumesegmenter-pipeline.md) by focusing on intensity-based foreground extraction and spatial partitioning rather than semantic segmentation, and from [3D Seismic Data Compression Pipeline](11727-3d-seismic-data-compression-pipeline.md) by using a generic intensity-threshold approach rather than seismic-specific physics [S1, unofficial].
+The Volume Partitioning Pipeline preprocesses 3D volumetric data by segmenting foreground regions, optionally partitioning them spatially or by intensity, cropping to bounding boxes, downsampling, and exporting as raw files with metadata [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of managing large, sparse, or complex volumes by reducing data size and organizing it into manageable components for downstream rendering or compression [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like the [VolumeSegmenter Pipeline](11584-volumesegmenter-pipeline.md) by explicitly handling partitioning strategies and raw export, and from the [3D Seismic Data Compression Pipeline](11727-3d-seismic-data-compression-pipeline.md) by focusing on general-purpose volume partitioning rather than seismic-specific compression [S1, unofficial].
 
